    "body": "AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n27 November 2026\nWiseTech Global has offered 16 major CargoWise customers an early move to its new pricing model, opening the way for it to charge for AI-driven labour savings. One has already signed, the company told shareholders at its AGM on Friday.\nWiseTech is starting with the 20 largest customers still on long-term contracts under its previous pricing model. This group represents just 3.7% of CargoWise customers but about 28% of its revenue, based on H1 FY26 figures. They are due to move automatically to CargoWise Value Packs when their deals expire, with about three-quarters of those contracts due to end by FY29.\n“We expect initial revenue on conversion to be neutral for us,” said CEO Zubin Appoo. Moving to Value Packs unlocks access to CargoWise’s AI agents, and WiseTech expects to earn more as customers meet agreed milestones for labour savings and efficiency. Co-founder Richard White said the company intended to charge “a tiny fraction of that labour saving”.\nWiseTech illustrated the potential with a calculation based on published staff costs. A 10% reduction could save some large forwarders US$180m-$300m a year, it said. The company did not disclose how it would measure savings for billing, or what share it would charge. It now has nine CargoWise AI agents live, up from six in August, with three more in pilot.\nAmong the forwarders to watch is DSV. In September, Mr Appoo told The Loadstar WiseTech had discussed moving DSV onto Value Packs before its contract expires in September 2028. DSV chief executive Jens Lund said he expected CargoWise and Tango, the forwarding platform DSV inherited from Schenker, to run alongside each other for some time. WiseTech did not name the customer that had already signed, or say whether DSV was among the 16.\nDespite a growing choice of forwarding software, and fears that competition would stymie CargoWise’s ability to attract new customers, it is still winning new business. WiseTech reported some 30% more new CargoWise signings in the first ten months this year than in the same period last year, with signings among small and medium-sized businesses up about 40%.\nCargoWise revenue also rose 11% year on year in the four months to October, helped by higher transaction volumes, growth from large global forwarders, and price increases on legacy contracts.\nWiseTech, which said its e2open integration is ahead of schedule, held its FY27 revenue guidance at US$1.48bn–$1.54bn. CFO Jeff Howard said trading to October gave the company confidence it could reach US$1.48bn–$1.5bn if momentum and favourable market conditions continued, before factoring-in uptake of early Value Pack conversions, paid AI features, and its VerifyWise supplier verification service.\nShareholder case and competition inquiry continue\nWiseTech also said the Australian Federal Police had closed its investigation into founder Mr White without taking action. The Australian Securities and Investments Commission has also closed its governance inquiry without enforcement action, and issued no-action letters to Mr White and two employees in a separate share-trading inquiry. The inquiry into alleged trading by a fourth individual continues.\nMeanwhile, the company noted that the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission’s investigation into the supply of global logistics services and software remained at an early stage.\nWiseTech is also defending a shareholder class action in the Supreme Court of Victoria. Investors allege the company misled the market and breached its disclosure obligations over expected earnings and potential acquisition payments in 2019 and 2020.\nWiseTech cut about 1,700 roles in FY26, mainly in product development and customer service. Its October employee survey put engagement at 68%, down from 76% in February 2025.",
